---
<PlaybookLayout
  title={title}
  description="Three 30-second methods for stress and anxiety, and three for getting more of the good stuff, from the Flourish workshop with Jon Krop."
  chapters={CHAPTERS}
  clientLookup={clientLookup}
>
  <!-- ============ Hero ============ -->
  <section class="hero" id="top">
    <div class="wide hero-in">
      <div>
        <img class="logo" src="/images/logo-orange.png" alt="Flourish Legal Well-Being" width="168" />
        <div class="backlink" id="backlink"><a></a></div>
        <h1>30-Second Stress Busters and Mood Boosters</h1>
        <p class="sub-title">Three ways to deal with the difficult stuff, and three ways to get more of the good stuff</p>
        <p class="from">A playbook from the workshop, with Jon Krop of <a href="https://teamflourish.com">Flourish</a></p>
      </div>
      <figure class="dial" aria-label="Short moments, many times">
        <div class="dial-ring">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 200 200" aria-hidden="true">
            <circle class="dial-track" cx="100" cy="100" r="88" />
            <circle class="dial-dash" cx="100" cy="100" r="88" pathLength="60" />
          </svg>
          <div class="dial-mid"><span class="dial-n">30</span><span class="dial-u">seconds</span></div>
        </div>
        <figcaption>
          <span class="dial-q">“Short moments, many times.”</span>
          <span class="dial-src">Advice my Tibetan meditation teachers gave all the time</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <main>
  <!-- ============ Intro ============ -->
  <section class="intro">
    <div class="col">
      <p class="big">Taking care of yourself doesn’t always need to be time-intensive. That’s good news, because at a law firm, sometimes you won’t have the time.</p>
      <p class="txt">If you have 15 minutes to meditate or an hour for a massage, I encourage that. For the rest of the time, here are the six methods from the workshop: three <b>stress busters</b> for the difficult stuff, and three <b>mood boosters</b> for getting more of the good stuff. Each one is doable in as little as 30 seconds, if that’s all the time you’ve got.</p>
      <p class="legend"><span class="chip" aria-hidden="true"></span>Lines in orange boxes like this are the exact words to say, out loud or in your head.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <nav class="contents" aria-label="The six methods">
    <div class="wide">
      <div class="groups">
        <div>
          <h2 class="grp">30-second stress busters</h2>
          <ol class="cards3">
            {BUSTERS.map((c) => (
              <li><a href={`#${c.id}`}><span class="n">{c.n}</span><span class="t">{c.t}</span></a></li>
            ))}
          </ol>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h2 class="grp">30-second mood boosters</h2>
          <ol class="cards3">
            {BOOSTERS.map((c) => (
              <li><a href={`#${c.id}`}><span class="n">{c.n}</span><span class="t">{c.t}</span></a></li>
            ))}
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="also">Plus two more tools, <a href="#tool-noting">deconstructing anxiety</a> and <a href="#tool-sleep">getting to sleep</a>, and some <a href="#reading">further reading</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </nav>

  <!-- ============ 1: Cognitive reframing ============ -->
  <section class="principle alt" id="reframe">
    <div class="col">
      <header class="ph"><span class="num" aria-hidden="true">1</span><h2>Reframe anxiety as excitement</h2></header>
      <p class="lead">When we get anxious, our natural response is to try to calm down. That’s hard to do, because being anxious and being calm are basically opposites.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="wide" style="max-width:calc(940px + 2*var(--gutter))">
      <div class="arousal" role="group" aria-label="Calm, anxiety and excitement compared">
        <div class="st calm">
          <b>Calm</b>
          <span class="lvl">Low arousal</span>
          <p>Muscles relaxed. Heart rate slow. Breathing slow.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hop far"><span class="ar" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>An enormous U-turn</span></div>
        <div class="st anx">
          <b>Anxiety</b>
          <span class="lvl">High arousal</span>
          <p>Muscles tense. Heart rate and breathing accelerated. Adrenaline and cortisol flooding your bloodstream.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hop near"><span class="ar" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Much closer</span></div>
        <div class="st exc">
          <b>Excitement</b>
          <span class="lvl">High arousal</span>
          <p>High levels of adrenaline. Elevated heart rate. Accelerated breathing.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="col">
      <ul class="pts" style="margin-top:36px">
        <li>Excitement feels in the body a lot like anxiety. That gives us an opportunity: <b>we can reinterpret the physical feelings of anxiety as feelings of excitement.</b></li>
      </ul>
      <div class="finding">
        <p>The idea comes from Alison Wood Brooks, a professor at Harvard Business School, who did a number of studies on this. When people reframed their anxiety as excitement, they:</p>
        <ul class="checks">
          <li>Performed better on anxiety-provoking tasks</li>
          <li>Felt more excited</li>
          <li>Tended to see challenges as opportunities rather than threats</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <ul class="pts">
        <li>Getting those benefits can be as simple as saying the words:</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="borrow-line">“I’m excited.”</p>
      <ul class="pts">
        <li>Out loud works if no one’s around (or if you’re feeling very bold). It works just as well if you say it quietly to yourself.</li>
        <li>Try it before a client meeting, or a feedback meeting you’re nervous about.</li>
      </ul>
      <aside class="jon">
        <img src="/images/jon-headshot-botanical.webp" alt="" width="600" height="744" loading="lazy" />
        <p>I do this before every presentation I give. I’ve been doing this for a long time, but I still get a little anxiety about public speaking. So I take a second, feel the little knot in the pit of my stomach, and say, “Oh, okay. I’m excited.” It’s not like I’m tricking myself. I know exactly what I’m doing. But as a technique, it’s effective.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ 2: Paced breathing ============ -->
  <section class="principle" id="breathe">
    <div class="col">
      <header class="ph"><span class="num" aria-hidden="true">2</span><h2>Paced breathing</h2></header>
      <p class="lead">It calms the mind and the body. It’s fast-acting, and it’s discreet: no one will know you’re doing it.</p>
      <ul class="pts">
        <li>You make a couple of simple adjustments to your breathing, and that stimulates a cranial nerve called the <b>vagus nerve</b>, which triggers a physiological relaxation response and calms the fight-or-flight response.</li>
        <li>There are just two core elements:</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="core2">
        <div class="core">
          <span class="mv">1</span>
          <h3>Breathe from the belly</h3>
          <p>When we’re stressed, we tend to breathe so that just the chest expands. Breathe all the way down instead, so your belly expands when you inhale. To check, rest a hand on your stomach and push it out with each inhale.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="core">
          <span class="mv">2</span>
          <h3>Slow it down</h3>
          <p>On average, people take 10 to 14 breaths a minute. Slowing to about six or fewer further stimulates the vagus nerve.</p>
          <div class="rate" aria-hidden="true">
            <div class="rate-row"><span class="dots">{Array.from({ length: 14 }, (_, i) => <i class={i >= 10 ? 'maybe' : ''}></i>)}</span><span class="rl">10 to 14 a minute</span></div>
            <div class="rate-row slow"><span class="dots">{Array.from({ length: 6 }, () => <i></i>)}</span><span class="rl">about 6</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 class="sub">Three patterns to try</h3>
      <p class="body">Which one feels most relaxing is a personal thing, so experiment. Keep the belly breathing going throughout, and count in seconds.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="wide" style="max-width:calc(820px + 2*var(--gutter))">
      <div class="patterns">
        <div class="pat">
          <div class="pat-h"><b>Equal segments</b><span>5 in, 5 out</span></div>
          <div class="cells" aria-hidden="true">
            <span class="seg in"><em>In</em>{Array.from({ length: 5 }, () => <i></i>)}</span>
            <span class="seg out"><em>Out</em>{Array.from({ length: 5 }, () => <i></i>)}</span>
          </div>
          <p class="pat-n">Inhale and exhale the same length.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pat">
          <div class="pat-h"><b>Lengthened exhale</b><span>4 in, 7 out</span></div>
          <div class="cells" aria-hidden="true">
            <span class="seg in"><em>In</em>{Array.from({ length: 4 }, () => <i></i>)}</span>
            <span class="seg out"><em>Out</em>{Array.from({ length: 7 }, () => <i></i>)}</span>
          </div>
          <p class="pat-n">Many people (not everyone) find a longer exhale deepens the calming effect.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pat boxpat">
          <div class="pat-h"><b>Box breathing</b><span>4 in, 4 hold, 4 out, 4 hold</span></div>
          <div class="box" aria-hidden="true">
            <span class="side t">Inhale 4</span>
            <span class="side r">Hold 4</span>
            <span class="side b">Exhale 4</span>
            <span class="side l">Hold 4</span>
          </div>
          <p class="pat-n">All four segments are the same length, like the four sides of a box.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="col">
      <p class="body" style="margin-top:28px">Once you have the basics, you can add a short, comfortable pause between the inhale and exhale, or bring some attention to the sensations of breathing, at either the nostrils or the abdomen.</p>
      <div class="watch">
        <p><b>How long does it take?</b> Many people find it works quickly, sometimes in as little as 30 seconds. The research appears to confirm shifts in how you feel in as little as five minutes.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="scene">
        <h3>Use it while your attention is somewhere else</h3>
        <p>Because it’s a purely physical practice, you can do it while you’re involved in things that require your attention. Say you’re in a meeting with opposing counsel, they’re being ridiculous, and you find yourself getting angry. You can’t turn inward and do a mindfulness exercise. You have to pay attention.</p>
        <p>But you can shift your breathing down into your belly, slow it down, and do a little paced breathing while keeping your focus on the conversation.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ 3: Am I sure that's true? ============ -->
  <section class="principle alt" id="sure">
    <div class="col">
      <header class="ph"><span class="num" aria-hidden="true">3</span><h2>“Am I sure that’s true?”</h2></header>
      <p class="lead">Emotions involve both thoughts in the mind and feelings in the body, and those two things feed one another.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="wide" style="max-width:calc(900px + 2*var(--gutter))">
      <div class="loop" role="group" aria-label="The thought-emotion loop">
        <div class="lp">An anxious or angry thought</div>
        <span class="la" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="lp">A feeling of anxiety or anger in the body</div>
        <span class="la" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="lp">Another flurry of thoughts about the feeling</div>
        <div class="lback"><span>And around you go</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="col">
      <ul class="pts" style="margin-top:36px">
        <li>What we often don’t realize is that our thoughts, especially our emotionally charged thoughts, are often distorted. <b>They feel true, but they’re not.</b></li>
        <li>If you can recognize that in the moment, you can break the loop. The emotions don’t go away. They just stop being disturbing and overwhelming.</li>
        <li>The next time you’re caught up in a negative or overwhelming thought, ask yourself:</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="borrow-line">“Am I sure that’s true?”</p>
      <ul class="pts">
        <li>Merely asking is often enough to loosen the thought’s hold, because it punctures the reflexive belief that our thoughts are always telling us the truth.</li>
        <li>If it’s not enough, add a follow-up question. Here are some good ones. They’re quite lawyerly:</li>
      </ul>
      <ol class="qs">
        <li>What evidence do I have that this thought is true?</li>
        <li>Is there any evidence this thought might not be true?</li>
        <li>Are there any assumptions hidden in this thought?</li>
        <li>Is there an alternative scenario that’s just as plausible?</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="wide" style="max-width:calc(940px + 2*var(--gutter))">
      <div class="story">
        <div class="story-txt">
          <h3>“Come to my office now.”</h3>
          <p>When I was a junior associate, I came into the office one morning and my phone buzzed in my pocket. It was an email from a partner on a case I was working on. It just said, “Come to my office now.”</p>
          <p>I started freaking out a little, and I managed to pack quite a lot of rumination into a short time.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="story-vis" aria-hidden="true">
          <div class="phone">
            <div class="note"><span class="from">Partner</span><span class="msg-t">Come to my office now.</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="thoughts">
            <span>What did I do?</span>
            <span>What did I mess up?</span>
            <span>I’m off the case.</span>
            <span>I’m never gonna make partner now.</span>
            <span>What if I’m fired?</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="story-end">
          <p><b>It was an absolute nothing.</b> Nothing was wrong. The partner just sent the email off quickly and didn’t think about how it would land with me. If I’d been able to stop and say, “Wait, wait, wait. Am I sure that’s true?” it would’ve saved me a lot of completely unnecessary anguish.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col">
      <h3 class="sub">Other moments it helps</h3>
      <ul class="pts">
        <li>You’re about to give feedback, and you’re expecting a ton of pushback.</li>
        <li>You gave feedback, and now you feel like you said all the wrong things.</li>
        <li>You just turned in a brief, and you’re terrified you missed a crucial case.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="yes">
        <h3>What if the answer is yes?</h3>
        <p>Sometimes there really is a problem. Then this technique won’t help much, because it is true. That’s why there are other techniques: paced breathing may help more. You can also ask another question:</p>
        <p class="borrow-line">“Am I sure this is as big a deal as it feels?”</p>
        <p>The answer to that one is often no. We human beings tend to catastrophize: we take an admittedly real problem and multiply it in our minds into a world-ending calamity that it is not.</p>
      </div>

      <details class="fold">
      <summary><span class="fold-in">Going further</span></summary>
      <ul class="pts">
        <li><b>Name the distortion.</b> A cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT) app, like Quirk CBT (iPhone) or What’s Up? (Android), lists the main cognitive distortions and walks you through the process.</li>
        <li><b>Replace the thought.</b> After challenging it, swap in a more helpful and less distorted one. For example:</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="swap">
        <p class="old"><s>“I’m going to mess up this presentation.”</s></p>
        <span class="swap-ar" aria-hidden="true">&darr;</span>
        <p class="new">“I can’t know how this presentation will go, but I do know I’ve prepared extensively, so I’ve set myself up to do a good job.”</p>
      </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ Part 2 band ============ -->
  <section class="part" aria-labelledby="part2">
    <div class="wide" style="max-width:calc(940px + 2*var(--gutter))">
      <h2 id="part2">30-second mood boosters</h2>
      <p class="part-lead">How do we get more of the good stuff? Here are three mental habits that research has shown to increase the experience of positive emotions and cultivate overall well-being. Conveniently, they all start with G.</p>
      <ol class="gs">
        <li><a href="#gratitude"><span class="g">G</span>ratitude</a></li>
        <li><a href="#generosity"><span class="g">G</span>enerosity</a></li>
        <li><a href="#goodwill"><span class="g">G</span>oodwill</a></li>
      </ol>
      <p class="part-txt">The same mental habits that bring us happiness and well-being also turn us into the sorts of people who bring others happiness and well-being.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ 4: Gratitude ============ -->
  <section class="principle" id="gratitude">
    <div class="col">
      <header class="ph"><span class="num" aria-hidden="true">4</span><h2>Gratitude</h2></header>
      <p class="lead">Studies indicate grateful people are happier, less stressed, and less prone to anxiety and depression. They cope better with problems, they’re more satisfied in their relationships, and they even sleep better. (That last one is the big draw for me.)</p>
      <ul class="pts">
        <li>You can cultivate gratitude through practice. Here are two simple methods, both popularized by Dr. Martin Seligman, the founder of positive psychology.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="sub">The gratitude journal</h3>
      <ul class="pts">
        <li>At the end of each day, write down <b>three things that went well</b> that day. Each one can be just a sentence.</li>
        <li>To make it more effective, also write down <b>why</b> each one happened, in a way that connects it to something positive about yourself.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="journal" role="group" aria-label="Example journal entry">
        <div class="jl"><span class="jn">1</span><div><span class="tag">Went well</span>I cooked a really nice dinner with my wife today.</div></div>
        <div class="jl why"><span class="jn"></span><div><span class="tag">Why</span>Because I made an intentional effort recently to find activities that we could do together.</div></div>
      </div>
      <ul class="pts">
        <li>Keep it up and it rewires your brain: you become more prone to noticing and appreciating the positive things in your life, and less prone to dwelling on the negative things.</li>
      </ul>
      <aside class="jon">
        <img src="/images/jon-headshot-botanical.webp" alt="" width="600" height="744" loading="lazy" />
        <p>I started keeping a gratitude journal during lockdown, and I’ve kept it up because the benefits were so obvious. I do it a little differently, in real time: a list in my phone’s notes app, broken up by day. Whenever something good happens, I jot it down right then, and I don’t limit myself to three. That’s not sanctioned by Dr. Seligman, but for me it’s been even better at training me to appreciate the good things as they happen.</p>
      </aside>
      <p class="quick"><b>The 30-second version:</b> whenever something good happens, jot it down right then.</p>

      <h3 class="sub">The gratitude visit</h3>
      <p class="body">This one isn’t as well known. It traditionally means visiting someone in person, which is probably the most powerful, but it works well over Zoom or the phone too.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="wide" style="max-width:calc(940px + 2*var(--gutter))">
      <ol class="steps4">
        <li><span class="mk">1</span><span>Think of someone who has shown you incredible kindness, ideally someone you’ve never fully thanked.</span></li>
        <li><span class="mk">2</span><span>Write them a short letter: what they did for you, how it affected you, how it made your life better. Try to express the full measure of your gratitude.</span></li>
        <li><span class="mk">3</span><span>Reach out and say you’d like to get together or catch up. You don’t have to explain why.</span></li>
        <li><span class="mk">4</span><span>When you’re together, tell them you have something you’d like to read to them. Then read the letter aloud.</span></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="col">
      <p class="fair">Fair warning: many people start crying, you or them. That’s okay. It’s a very powerful practice.</p>

      <p class="cmp-lead">Here’s what the studies suggest:</p>
      <div class="compare">
        <div class="cmp">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 120 50" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 46 L4 46 Q20 46 26 6 Q32 46 116 46" /></svg>
          <b>The visit</b><span>leads to a bigger boost in happiness</span>
        </div>
        <div class="cmp">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 120 50" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 46 Q14 22 30 22 L116 22" /></svg>
          <b>The journal</b><span>has longer-lasting benefits</span>
        </div>
        <p class="cmp-end">Luckily, you can just do both.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ 5: Generosity ============ -->
  <section class="principle alt" id="generosity">
    <div class="col">
      <header class="ph"><span class="num" aria-hidden="true">5</span><h2>Generosity</h2></header>
      <p class="lead">There are ancient roots to the understanding that being generous toward other people is good not just for them, but for you.</p>
      <blockquote class="old-q">
        <p>“If beings knew, as I know, the results of giving and sharing, they would not eat without having given.”</p>
        <footer>Siddhartha Gautama, the Buddha. Whenever he taught ordinary people, his first lesson was never about meditation. It was always about generosity.</footer>
      </blockquote>
      <ul class="pts">
        <li>Modern science reinforces this. Giving time, effort, money, or items is associated with <b>better physical health and even a longer lifespan</b>, and a large body of research shows it improves well-being and even mental health.</li>
        <li>I give to nonprofits and causes that need financial support. I also try to give to someone specific, because it comes with a nice little bonus:</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="card bonus">
        <h3>Give to someone specific</h3>
        <p>Research indicates that <b>targeted giving</b>, to identifiable individuals rather than a broad class of people, yields an additional benefit: it’s associated with reduced activity in the amygdala, and with decreases in anxiety.</p>
      </div>
      <aside class="jon" style="margin-top:32px">
        <img src="/images/jon-headshot-botanical.webp" alt="" width="600" height="744" loading="lazy" />
        <p>I live in New York City, and when I take the subway, there are a lot of people who are having a hard time and might need something to eat. So I buy a 30-pack of Clif Bars and make sure I always have two in my bag. If I meet someone asking for help, I always have something I can give. Is that going to solve homelessness in New York City? No. That’s why I also give to organizations. I do both the systemic stuff and the individual stuff.</p>
      </aside>
      <p class="quick"><b>The 30-second version:</b> one small act of giving, like handing someone a Clif Bar.</p>
      <ul class="pts">
        <li><b>Pro bono</b> is an incredible way to engage in targeted giving, combining it with your work at the firm and using your skills as an attorney.</li>
        <li>Sharing what you do gives other people ideas, so sharing is itself an act of generosity. A few from the chat at a recent session:</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="chat2" role="group" aria-label="Acts of generosity shared in a session chat">
        <div class="chat-bar"><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>Chat</div>
        <div class="feed">
          <span class="m">I make sure to either make tea for my wife in the morning or at least boil the jug before I leave.</span>
          <span class="m">I cook and share meals with others.</span>
          <span class="m">I donate blood.</span>
          <span class="m">I sometimes pay for the groceries of the person behind me just randomly.</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="yes">
        <h3>What about burnout?</h3>
        <p>You never want to practice generosity at the expense of your own well-being. You can’t pour from an empty cup. Joseph Goldstein, one of the great living meditation teachers in the West, describes a sweet spot: <b>give a little more than is easy, and stop there.</b></p>
        <p>He also suggests an experiment. Take one day where, whenever you think about giving (grabbing something from the printer for someone, giving a buck to someone on the street), you stop thinking and just do it. I’ve tried it. It’s kind of fun.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ 6: Goodwill ============ -->
  <section class="principle" id="goodwill">
    <div class="col">
      <header class="ph"><span class="num" aria-hidden="true">6</span><h2>Goodwill</h2></header>
      <p class="lead">By goodwill, I mean a specific mental state: the heartfelt wish for others to be happy.</p>
      <ul class="pts">
        <li>Research indicates that goodwill practices increase positive emotions and appear to improve well-being, <b>even when you’re confronted with the distress of others.</b></li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="sub">On the go</h3>
      <ul class="pts">
        <li>Whenever you encounter people, in person or virtually, silently wish them well by saying in your mind:</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="borrow-line">“May you be happy.”</p>
      <ul class="pts">
        <li>In a meeting or on a video call, say it in your mind for the people in it. Walking down the hallway past colleagues, or past total strangers on the street: “May you be happy.”</li>
        <li>It takes two seconds each time and won’t interrupt your day. But encounter by encounter, day by day, you’re making it more your default.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="watch" style="margin-top:24px"><div class="wk">When to use it</div><ul>
        <li>Before or after a contentious call, meeting, or negotiation</li>
        <li>When you find yourself getting angry or frustrated with adversaries, clients, or colleagues</li>
        <li>When your mood is low or you’re feeling down</li>
      </ul></div>

      <div class="listen">
        <div class="listen-h"><b>Guided goodwill meditation</b><span>About 11 minutes, guided by Jon</span></div>
        <audio controls preload="metadata" src="/uploads/goodwill-meditation-guided.mp3">
          <a href="/uploads/goodwill-meditation-guided.mp3">Play the guided goodwill meditation</a>
        </audio>
      </div>
    </div>

    <details class="fold fold-full" id="full-practice">
      <summary class="col"><span class="fold-in">The full practice, step by step</span></summary>
      <div class="col">
        <p class="body">Practice this even occasionally and it will deepen your capacity for goodwill. You don’t have to feel any particular way while you do it. It’s more about planting seeds in the mind that ripen in time.</p>
        <p class="body">Sit comfortably, and close your eyes if you like. Bring each person to mind in turn, reflect that they’re a human being just like you, doing their best to find some happiness and doing their best not to suffer, and silently repeat:</p>
        <p class="borrow-line phrases">{PHRASES.map((p) => <span>“{p}”</span>)}</p>
        <p class="body">Then drop the words and just sit for a moment with the attitude of well-wishing.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="wide" style="max-width:calc(940px + 2*var(--gutter))">
        <ol class="steps4 who">
          <li><span class="mk">1</span><span><b>Someone you love.</b> Someone who brings up a lot of warm feelings. A cute baby works well. So do pets.</span></li>
          <li><span class="mk">2</span><span><b>Someone neutral.</b> A coworker you don’t know well, or the person who hands you your coffee in the morning.</span></li>
          <li><span class="mk">3</span><span><b>Someone you’ve had challenges with.</b> You don’t need to pick your worst enemy. Just someone you’ve had some issues with from time to time.</span></li>
          <li><span class="mk">4</span><span><b>Yourself.</b> It can help to imagine yourself as a small child.</span></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="col">
        <p class="body" style="margin-top:28px">For yourself, the words change:</p>
        <p class="borrow-line">“As I am no less deserving than anyone else, may I be happy. May I be healthy. May I be safe. May I be at peace.”</p>
        <ul class="pts">
          <li>You can also add a round for everyone on earth.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </details>

    <div class="col">
      <figure class="wq">
        <figcaption>The Dalai Lama likes to say:</figcaption>
        <blockquote>“If you want to be selfish, be wisely selfish: care for others.”</blockquote>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ More tools ============ -->
  <section class="toolkit" id="more">
    <div class="wide" style="max-width:calc(980px + 2*var(--gutter))">
      <details class="fold fold-big">
      <summary><h2 class="fold-in">Two more tools: for anxiety and for sleep</h2></summary>

      <div class="tool" id="tool-noting">
        <div class="tool-meta">
          <h3>Deconstructing anxiety</h3>
          <p class="meta-d">A simple, powerful mindfulness practice that doubles as a method for working with anxiety.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="tool-body">
          <p>Let your attention float freely. As it drifts, various sights, sounds, sensations, and thoughts will grab it and stand out. As this happens, notice whatever stands out most, and give it a light mental label:</p>
          <ul class="labels">
            <li><b>“Seeing”</b><span>for sights</span></li>
            <li><b>“Hearing”</b><span>for sounds</span></li>
            <li><b>“Feeling”</b><span>for physical sensations</span></li>
            <li><b>“Thinking”</b><span>for anything mental</span></li>
            <li><b>“Don’t know”</b><span>when you’re not sure what’s standing out</span></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="tool-wide two">
          <div class="watch"><div class="wk">Why it helps</div><ul>
            <li>Accessible even when the mind is agitated or unable to focus</li>
            <li>Easy to do</li>
            <li>Well suited to short bursts of on-the-go practice</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="watch"><div class="wk">When to use it</div><ul>
            <li>Any time you feel anxious or overwhelmed: before depositions, court appearances, client pitches, business development opportunities, high-pressure calls or meetings</li>
          </ul></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="tool" id="tool-sleep">
        <div class="tool-meta">
          <h3>Getting to sleep</h3>
          <p class="meta-d">As with anxiety, resisting sleeplessness only makes it worse.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="tool-body">
          <ul class="principles3">
            <li>Being less concerned with falling asleep will help you sleep better.</li>
            <li>You can’t will yourself to become sleepy.</li>
          </ul>
          <h4 class="rule-h">The half-hour rule</h4>
          <ol class="cycle">
            <li>If you’re not falling asleep, don’t lie there and “keep trying.” It will only stress you.</li>
            <li>After roughly 20 to 30 minutes (don’t watch a clock), get up.</li>
            <li>Go into a different room, do something relaxing for 20 to 30 minutes, then go back to bed.</li>
            <li>If you become sleepy, great. If not, don’t worry about it. Just repeat as many times as you need.</li>
          </ol>
          <p class="remember"><b>Remember:</b> missing sleep is very common and isn’t a big deal.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      </details>

      <div class="reading" id="reading">
        <h3>Further reading</h3>
        <p>Research and outside resources behind the workshop, to download.</p>
        <ul>
          {READING.map((r) => (
